----ENDF/B-VII        MATERIAL 6500                               6500 1451    7
-----ATOMIC RELAXATION DATA                                       6500 1451    8
------ENDF-6 FORMAT                                               6500 1451    9
==================================================================6500 1451   10
The Livermore Evaluated Atomic Data Library (EADL) in the         6500 1451   11
ENDF-6 Format. Translated from the Livermore ENDL format          6500 1451   12
to the ENDF-6 Format by Bob MacFarlane (Los Alamos).              6500 1451   13
==================================================================6500 1451   14
  Contents                                                        6500 1451   15
==================================================================6500 1451   16
  MF/MT    Description                                            6500 1451   17
==================================================================6500 1451   18
  28/533   Atomic Relaxation Data for Electrons and Photons       6500 1451   19
==================================================================6500 1451   20
  Warning                                                         6500 1451   21
==================================================================6500 1451   22
  MF/MT combinations used to define ALL electron interaction data 6500 1451   23
  are newly defined MF/MT numbers for ENDF/B-VI that did not exist6500 1451   24
  in earlier versions of ENDF/B.                                  6500 1451   25
==================================================================6500 1451   26
  Definition of Data                                              6500 1451   27
==================================================================6500 1451   28
  Atomic Relaxation Data for Electrons and Photons                6500 1451   29
==================================================================6500 1451   30
1) An atom can be ionized by a photon or an electron interaction. 6500 1451   31
2) The atomic relaxation data included here describes how a       6500 1451   32
   singly ionized atom returns to neutrality, by emitting photons 6500 1451   33
   (x-rays) and/or electrons (Auger, Coster-Kronig).              6500 1451   34
3) Data is given to define the transition probabilities between   6500 1451   35
   subshells for radiative (x-ray emission) and non-radiative     6500 1451   36
   (Auger, Coster-Kronig) transitions.                            6500 1451   37
4) The complete spectrum of emitted photons and electrons can be  6500 1451   38
   calculated using the data included here, e.g., see ref. 2,     6500 1451   39
   below, for am example of a computer code that analytically     6500 1451   40
   calculates these spectra.                                      6500 1451   41
==================================================================6500 1451   42
  References                                                      6500 1451   43
==================================================================6500 1451   44
1) S.T. Perkins, D.E. Cullen, etal., "Tables and Graphs of Atomic 6500 1451   45
   Subshell and Relaxation Data Derived from the LLNL Evaluated   6500 1451   46
   Atomic Data Library (EADL), Z = 1 - 100", UCRL-50400, Vol. 30, 6500 1451   47
   Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory (1991).                 6500 1451   48
2) D.E. Cullen "Program RELAX: A Code Designed to Calculate X-Ray 6500 1451   49
   and Electron Emission Spectra as Singly Charged Atoms Relax    6500 1451   50
   Back to Neutrality", UCRL-ID-110438, Lawrence Livermore        6500 1451   51
   National Laboratory (1992).                                    6500 1451   52
                                                                  6500 1451   53
  These are the primary references to the contents of this library6500 1451   54
  and they contain a complete list of references to the sources   6500 1451   55
  of data used in this library, methods of evaluation, accuracy   6500 1451   56
  of the data, etc.                                               6500 1451   57
==================================================================6500 1451   58
  History                                                         6500 1451   59
==================================================================6500 1451   60
 (1) November, 1991 - Initial release in the ENDL format.         6500 1451   61
 (2) October,  2001 - Initial release in the ENDF-6 format.       6500 1451   62
